Here's the honest maths: $4,200 − $3,600 leaves about $600 a month, which is $7,200 over 12 months — about $2,800 short of $10,000. So with today's numbers the goal is likely out of reach, but the gap is small enough to close. To hit $10k you'd need about $835 a month instead of $600, i.e. an extra ~$235 per month.
Options — you pick:
• trim about $235/month from expenses (the plan would start by reviewing the last 2 months of spending);
• add some extra income;
• keep saving $600/month and reach $10k in about 17 months;
• keep the date and aim for about $7,200.
Which way do you want to go?

Можно, для подтягиваний зал не обязателен. Предлагаю так:
• турник — дверной или на ближайшей спортплощадке;
• вместо тренажёров — негативные подтягивания (медленное опускание) и подтягивания с резиновой петлёй;
• 3 тренировки в неделю по 25–30 минут.
Если берёте дверной турник, проверьте, что дверной проём крепкий и перекладина держит ваш вес, — это важно для безопасности. Заменить задачи?

Congratulations on the job! At 3 hours a week the unfinished part of the plan (about 115 hours) would take around 38 weeks — roughly 9 months, well past March 1 — so something has to give. My proposal:
• drop "Learn D3 from scratch" and the blog posts — they're nice-to-haves;
• make project 3 a smaller one in the tools you already use (about 15 hours instead of 30);
• keep March 1 for projects 2 and 3 plus a simple one-page portfolio site (about 8 hours instead of 15).
That leaves about 48 hours of work: about 16 weeks at 3 h/week, so roughly 6 weeks of buffer before March 1. If you'd rather keep the D3 project, the alternative is moving the deadline to early May. Which do you prefer?
